{"task_id": "Mbpp/142", "solution": "def compare_at_position(list1, list2, list3, index):\n    \"\"\"Compares elements at a specific position across three lists.\n    Returns True if all three elements are identical.\"\"\"\n    return list1[index] == list2[index] and list2[index] == list3[index]\n\ndef count_samepair(list1, list2, list3):\n    \"\"\"Counts number items that are identical in the same position of three given lists.\"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    # The problem statement and design context imply that input lists will be of equal length.\n    for i in range(len(list1)):\n        if compare_at_position(list1, list2, list3, i):\n            count += 1\n    return count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/160", "solution": "def find_solution(a: int, b: int, n: int) -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds integer solutions for the linear Diophantine equation ax + by = n.\n    Returns solution (x, y) if it exists, None otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _gcd(x, y):\n        \"\"\"Calculates the greatest common divisor using the Euclidean algorithm.\"\"\"\n        while y:\n            x, y = y, x % y\n        return x\n\n    def _extended_euclidean(x, y):\n        \"\"\"Extended Euclidean algorithm to find coefficients s, t such that xs + yt = gcd(x, y).\"\"\"\n        if x == 0:\n            return y, 0, 1\n        gcd, s1, t1 = _extended_euclidean(y % x, x)\n        s = t1 - (y // x) * s1\n        t = s1\n        return gcd, s, t\n\n    # Handle cases where a or b are zero\n    if a == 0 and b == 0:\n        return (0, 0) if n == 0 else None\n    if a == 0:\n        if n % b == 0:\n            return (0, n // b)\n        else:\n            return None\n    if b == 0:\n        if n % a == 0:\n            return (n // a, 0)\n        else:\n            return None\n\n    gcd_val = _gcd(a, b)\n\n    if n % gcd_val != 0:\n        return None\n\n    # Scale factor for the particular solution\n    k = n // gcd_val\n\n    # Apply Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find a_prime * x_prime + b_prime * y_prime = gcd_val\n    # where a_prime and b_prime are a and b\n    gcd_found, x_particular, y_particular = _extended_euclidean(a, b)\n\n    # Scale the particular solution to match n\n    x_solution = x_particular * k\n    y_solution = y_particular * k\n\n    return (x_solution, y_solution)"}
