{"task_id": "Mbpp/235", "solution": "def even_bit_set_number(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets all even-positioned bits (0, 2, 4, etc.) to 1 in the given number.\n    This is achieved by performing a bitwise OR with a mask of all even bits.\n    \"\"\"\n    # The mask 0x5555555555555555 has 1s at all even bit positions\n    # (0, 2, 4, ..., 62) and 0s at all odd bit positions within a 64-bit range.\n    # Python's arbitrary precision integers handle this gracefully by\n    # effectively ORing the number with this mask, treating the mask as\n    # having leading zeros if the number is larger.\n    even_bits_mask = 0x5555555555555555\n    \n    # Perform a bitwise OR operation to set all even bits of the number to 1.\n    result = number | even_bits_mask\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/235", "solution": "def even_bit_set_number(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets all even-positioned bits (0, 2, 4, ...) of the given number to 1.\n    This operation uses a bitwise OR with a mask of all even bits set.\n    \"\"\"\n    # A mask with 1s at all even bit positions (0, 2, 4, 6, etc.).\n    # For typical integer sizes, this covers up to 64 bits.\n    # 0x5555555555555555 in binary is a repeating pattern of 0101...0101.\n    even_bits_mask = 0x5555555555555555\n    \n    # Perform a bitwise OR operation.\n    # This sets any even-positioned bit to 1 if it is currently 0,\n    # and leaves it as 1 if it is already 1. Odd-positioned bits remain unchanged.\n    return number | even_bits_mask"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/237", "solution": "def check_occurences(tuple_list: list) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"\n    Takes a list of tuples and returns a dictionary mapping each unique normalized tuple\n    to its occurrence count. Tuples are normalized by sorting their elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    normalized_tuples = []\n    for original_tuple in tuple_list:\n        # Normalize the tuple by sorting its elements and converting back to a tuple.\n        normalized_tuple = tuple(sorted(original_tuple))\n        normalized_tuples.append(normalized_tuple)\n\n    occurrence_counts = {}\n    for n_tuple in normalized_tuples:\n        # Count occurrences of each normalized tuple.\n        occurrence_counts[n_tuple] = occurrence_counts.get(n_tuple, 0) + 1\n\n    return occurrence_counts"}
