{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of possible sequences of length n, such that each element\n    is a positive integer, greater than or equal to twice the previous element,\n    and less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    # dp[k][val] stores the number of valid sequences of length k ending with 'val'.\n    # prefix_sums[k][val] stores the sum of dp[k][i] for i from 1 to val.\n    # This allows efficient calculation of dp[k][current_val] by looking up prefix_sums[k-1][current_val // 2].\n    \n    # Initialize dp and prefix_sums tables. Dimensions (n+1) x (m+1) to use 1-based indexing.\n    dp = [[0 for _ in range(m + 1)] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    prefix_sums = [[0 for _ in range(m + 1)] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    # Base case: For sequences of length 1, any positive integer 'val' up to 'm' is valid.\n    for val in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp[1][val] = 1\n    \n    # Calculate prefix sums for length 1.\n    for val in range(1, m + 1):\n        prefix_sums[1][val] = prefix_sums[1][val - 1] + dp[1][val]\n\n    # Fill the DP table for lengths from 2 to n.\n    for k in range(2, n + 1):\n        for current_val in range(1, m + 1):\n            # The previous element (prev_val) must satisfy prev_val <= current_val // 2.\n            # Also, prev_val must be a positive integer, so prev_val >= 1.\n            max_prev_val = current_val // 2\n            \n            if max_prev_val >= 1:\n                # The number of sequences of length k-1 that can precede current_val\n                # is the sum of dp[k-1][prev_val] for prev_val from 1 to max_prev_val.\n                # This sum is efficiently obtained from prefix_sums[k-1][max_prev_val].\n                dp[k][current_val] = prefix_sums[k - 1][max_prev_val]\n        \n        # Calculate prefix sums for the current length k.\n        for val in range(1, m + 1):\n            prefix_sums[k][val] = prefix_sums[k][val - 1] + dp[k][val]\n\n    # The total number of sequences of length n is the sum of dp[n][val] for all possible 'val'.\n    # This sum is stored in prefix_sums[n][m].\n    return prefix_sums[n][m]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of sequences of length n with elements up to m,\n    where each element is at least twice the previous one.\n    \"\"\"\n    # dp_prev stores the counts of valid sequences of length `i-1` ending with value `j`.\n    # dp_curr stores the counts for the current length `i`.\n    # We use 1-based indexing for values (from 1 to m), so array size is m + 1.\n    dp_prev = [0] * (m + 1)\n\n    # Base case: For sequences of length 1, any number from 1 to m is valid.\n    for j in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp_prev[j] = 1\n\n    # Iterate for sequence lengths from 2 up to n.\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        dp_curr = [0] * (m + 1)\n        prefix_sum_of_prev_row = 0\n        # k_limit tracks the maximum value for the previous element such that it's <= j // 2.\n        k_limit = 0 \n\n        # Iterate through possible values for the current element `j` (from 1 to m).\n        for j in range(1, m + 1):\n            # The previous element `k` must satisfy `2 * k <= j`, i.e., `k <= j // 2`.\n            # We efficiently maintain `prefix_sum_of_prev_row` by adding `dp_prev[k]`\n            # as `j // 2` increases.\n            while k_limit + 1 <= j // 2:\n                k_limit += 1\n                prefix_sum_of_prev_row += dp_prev[k_limit]\n            \n            # The number of sequences of current length ending with `j`\n            # is the sum of sequences of previous length ending with `k`\n            # where `k` is valid.\n            dp_curr[j] = prefix_sum_of_prev_row\n        \n        # Update dp_prev for the next length iteration.\n        dp_prev = dp_curr  \n\n    # The total number of sequences of length n is the sum of all valid sequences\n    # ending with any value from 1 to m.\n    return sum(dp_prev)"}
