{"task_id": "Mbpp/414", "solution": "def overlapping(seq1, seq2) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if any element from seq1 exists in seq2.\n    Uses a set for efficient lookup, providing O(n+m) time complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not seq1 or not seq2:\n        return False\n\n    set2 = set(seq2)\n    for item in seq1:\n        if item in set2:\n            return True\n    return False"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "def max_Product(array: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    # Finds the pair of integers with the highest product from the given array.\n    # It iterates through all unique pairs, updating the maximum product and corresponding pair as it goes.\n    n = len(array)\n    \n    # Initialize max_product with the product of the first two elements\n    # and result_pair with these two elements. This assumes n >= 2 based on design constraints.\n    max_product = array[0] * array[1]\n    result_pair = (array[0], array[1])\n    \n    # Iterate through all possible unique pairs of elements\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            current_product = array[i] * array[j]\n            \n            # If the current product is greater than the maximum product found so far, update them\n            if current_product > max_product:\n                max_product = current_product\n                result_pair = (array[i], array[j])\n                \n    return result_pair"}
